{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>In the third month</b> — It would have been fitting for Scripture to say: \"They journeyed from Rephidim and encamped in the wilderness of Sinai in the third month after their departure from the land of Egypt,\" just as it said above (Exodus 16:1) regarding the wilderness of Sin. But because their arrival at the wilderness of Sinai was a joy for them and a festival, and from the time of their departure from Egypt they longed for it — for they knew that there they would receive the Torah, since Moses had told them what was said to him: <b>you shall serve Elohim upon this mountain</b> (Exodus 3:12), and he also said to Pharaoh: <b>Let us go, please, a three days' journey into the wilderness and sacrifice to YHWH our Elohim</b> (Exodus 5:3), and that is the journey from Egypt to Mount Sinai — therefore the pericope began with <b>In the third month, on this day</b>, meaning that at the beginning of the month they came there, and afterward He returned to state the matter as with all the other journeys: <b>They journeyed from Rephidim</b>.\n\nIt would also have been fitting for it to say \"They journeyed from Rephidim and encamped in the wilderness of Sinai,\" but it wrote <b>they came into the wilderness of Sinai</b>, to say that as soon as they came into the wilderness of Sinai they encamped in the wilderness when they saw the mountain opposite them, and they did not wait until they entered it to find a good place to encamp there; rather, they encamped in the wilderness or in Horev, which is a place of desolation and wasteland, in front of the mountain. And this is the meaning of <b>they encamped in the wilderness, and Israel encamped there opposite the mountain</b>. And it is possible that they separated from among themselves all the mixed multitude that was in their midst, and Israel alone encamped before the mountain, with the mixed multitude behind them, for to Israel would He give the Torah, as He said (Exodus 19:2), \"Thus shall you say to the house of Jacob and declare to the children of Israel.\" And this is the meaning of \"Israel encamped there,\" or else it is to mention them in a manner of honor when they received the Torah.\n\nRashi wrote (Rashi on Exodus 19:2): \"'They journeyed from Rephidim' — why was it necessary to go back and explain from where they journeyed? Was it not already stated (Exodus 17:1) that they were encamped at Rephidim? It is known that from there they journeyed. Rather, it juxtaposes their journeying from Rephidim to their encampment in the wilderness of Sinai: just as their encampment was in repentance, so too their journeying was in repentance\" — thus far Rashi's language. And I did not understand this, for thus it is said in all the journeys: \"they encamped at Elim\"; \"they journeyed from Elim and came into the wilderness of Sin\" (Exodus 16:1); \"they journeyed from the wilderness of Sin and encamped at Rephidim\" (Exodus 17:1), and so too the entire pericope of Mas'ei (Numbers 33). Scripture wishes to explain that there were no other encampments between them.\n\nAnd the language of Mekhilta (here): <b>They journeyed from Rephidim and came into the wilderness of Sinai</b> — was it not already stated in the pericope of journeys (Numbers 33:15) \"they journeyed from Rephidim and encamped in the wilderness of Sinai\"? What does it teach by saying <b>they came into the wilderness of Sinai</b>? It juxtaposes their journeying from Rephidim, etc. And the point is to say that since all the journeys that He recounted here — from Elim (Exodus 16:1) and Rephidim (Exodus 17:1) — were repeated in the pericope of Eileh Mas'ei on account of matters that were newly taught there in that passage, but this journey Scripture recounted here and there equally, it was necessary to derive through midrash that it served the purpose of the juxtaposition."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:17:23.935205105Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}